/// \defgroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def W3D particle emitter definition: the in-memory form of a W3D emitter chunk
/// \ingroup grp_w3d_rendering
/// \brief ParticleEmitterDefClass, the editable description of one W3D
///        particle emitter: construction and copying, the setters and
///        keyframe tracks, the volume randomizers, and the parts of its W3D
///        chunk reader and writer that handle the keyframe properties and
///        the extra-info record.
///
/// A W3D file can hold, besides meshes, hierarchies and animations, a
/// particle emitter, stored as one emitter chunk. ParticleEmitterDefClass is
/// that chunk in memory. It is a description, not a render object: a texture
/// name, emission settings, a volume to spawn in, a velocity spread, a
/// shader and six keyframe tracks. Nothing in it emits, moves or draws a
/// particle. ParticleEmitterClass, the render object, does that.
///
/// \par How a definition meets the emitter
/// - ParticleEmitterClass::Create_From_Definition reads a definition through
///   its getters and builds a live emitter. The values it passes on include
///   the texture name, shader, emission rate, burst size, the two volume
///   randomizers, velocity and acceleration, outward velocity, velocity
///   inheritance, the six tracks and the initial orientation random value,
///   lifetime, future start time, maximum emissions, render mode, frame mode
///   and line properties. It also copies the definition's name onto the new
///   emitter with Set_Name. It does not read the definition's gravity,
///   elasticity or fade time.
/// - ParticleEmitterClass::Build_Definition goes the other way. It makes a
///   new definition and copies a live emitter's settings in through the
///   setters documented here, then returns it to the caller, who owns it.
///   ParticleEmitterClass::Save is that call followed by
///   ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_W3D. Gravity and elasticity are always
///   set to zero on this path.
/// - Zero Hour: the asset manager picks the registered prototype loader
///   whose chunk type matches each chunk it finds, and
///   ParticleEmitterLoaderClass handles the emitter chunk type. For each
///   emitter chunk it is given, that loader creates a definition, asks it to
///   load, and wraps a successful one in a ParticleEmitterPrototypeClass. That prototype
///   creates the emitter with ParticleEmitterClass::Create_From_Definition
///   and deletes the definition when the prototype dies. Zero Hour
///   registers the loader in W3DDisplay::init (and in the WorldBuilder
///   view). Where BFME 2 registers it is not established here.
///
/// \par Not the game's particle effects
/// The game's own particle effects are a different system. They are
/// FXParticleSystem blocks in INI, built from emitter, volume, velocity,
/// update and draw modules (\ref grp_w3_fx_particle_modules, \ref sub_gameclient).
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass is the asset-side path: a particle emitter stored
/// inside a .w3d file as an emitter chunk. Zero Hour: such an emitter is
/// created by name through the asset manager, like a model, by a prototype
/// class and a loader class (see above). BFME 2's use of those two classes is
/// not established here. What the BFME 2 bodies show is narrower: a
/// definition is built from a live ParticleEmitterClass and turned back into
/// one (ParticleEmitterClass::Build_Definition and
/// ParticleEmitterClass::Create_From_Definition), and it is saved and loaded
/// as a W3D emitter chunk.
///
/// \par Keyframe tracks
/// Six tracks describe how a particle changes over its life: colour,
/// opacity, size, rotation, texture frame and blur time. Each is a
/// ParticlePropertyStruct: a start value, a random-variation amount, a count
/// of further keyframes, and two parallel arrays holding their times and
/// values. The start value is kept outside the arrays. On disk it is the
/// first record, with time zero, so a colour, opacity or size track with N
/// further keyframes is written as N plus one records, and the counts in the
/// properties record are N plus one (ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Props
/// adds the one; the reader takes it off again). Colour, opacity and size
/// share one properties chunk; the other three tracks are written to chunks
/// of their own.
///
/// \par Chunk order
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_W3D, whose BFME 2 body returns a bool,
/// writes one emitter chunk holding these chunks in this order: header, user
/// data, info, version 2 info, properties, line properties, rotation
/// keyframes, frame keyframes, blur-time keyframes and extra info. The
/// BFME 2 loader, ParticleEmitterDefClass::Load_W3D, expects the header,
/// the user data and the info chunk first, in exactly that order, and for a
/// file above version 1.0 the version 2 info and the properties after them.
/// It then takes whatever chunks follow in any order, dispatches the five
/// known ones (line properties and the rotation, frame, blur-time and extra
/// info chunks), and skips any other chunk id silently: the BFME 2 body
/// closes the chunk and goes on to the next one, with no message. (Zero
/// Hour's source logs "Unhandled Chunk" there, but only in builds with debug
/// logging enabled.) A file at version 1.0 or lower has no version 2 chunks,
/// and ParticleEmitterDefClass::Convert_To_Ver2 derives the version 2 values
/// from its version 1 info instead. Files are written as version 2.0.
///
/// \par Differences from Zero Hour
/// Established by comparing the BFME 2 bodies with Zero Hour's part_ldr.cpp.
/// This is not a complete list of the differences between the two:
/// - The members that load and save a whole definition or one of its chunks
///   return a bool in BFME 2, where Zero Hour's return WW3DErrorType
///   (WW3D_ERROR_OK and the failure codes). Among the members documented
///   here that covers ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Props,
///   ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Extra_Info and the colour, opacity and size
///   writers; the same holds for Load_W3D, Save_W3D, the chunk-level Read_
///   members (header, user data, info, version 2 info, properties, line
///   properties, the rotation, frame and blur-time keyframes, and extra info)
///   and the other Save_ members. It does not apply to the per-key readers
///   Read_Color_Keyframe, Read_Opacity_Keyframe and Read_Size_Keyframe, which
///   return a bool in Zero Hour as well.
/// - ParticleEmitterDefClass::Convert_To_Ver2 chooses the shader of a version
///   1 file differently. Zero Hour's source starts from the additive sprite
///   preset and switches to the alpha sprite preset when Has_Alpha accepts
///   the texture's format. The BFME 2 body has the same two steps, but its
///   test is a call to a texture-handle predicate that always returns false,
///   so a converted version 1 emitter always ends with the additive sprite
///   preset.
/// - The constructors, assignment, Free_Props, the track setters, the
///   filename setters, Initialize_To_Ver2, the randomizer conversion and
///   the colour reader do what Zero Hour's do.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Everything a definition holds is data in the .w3d emitter chunk. No
///   INI field of this class is established.
/// - Initialize_To_Ver2 stores placeholder values before a file is read: a
///   burst of one particle, the additive sprite shader preset, zero outward
///   velocity and velocity inheritance, and solid boxes of zero size for both
///   volumes. They are not defaults that a loaded emitter keeps. A version 2
///   file's version 2 info chunk replaces the whole record, and a version 2
///   file that lacks that chunk fails to load rather than falling back to
///   them. A version 1 file gets its version 2 values from
///   ParticleEmitterDefClass::Convert_To_Ver2 instead (a burst of one, the
///   additive sprite preset, and volumes derived from the version 1 position
///   and velocity randomness). In BFME 2 the conversion always ends with the
///   additive sprite preset, because the texture-alpha predicate it calls
///   always returns false; the Zero Hour source chooses the alpha sprite
///   preset from the texture format instead.
///   Fields the conversion does not set, such as the render mode and frame
///   mode of a version 1 file, keep the zero fill.
/// - A volume is one of four shapes: solid box, solid sphere, hollow sphere
///   and solid cylinder, identified in the file by the ids 0 to 3 in that
///   order. Ids are part of the format; do not renumber them. An unknown id
///   yields no randomizer, and the header's inline volume getters clone
///   through the pointer without testing it, so an asset with an unknown id
///   is not a supported input (ParticleEmitterDefClass::Create_Randomizer).
/// - The emitter's name is saved into a fixed-size name field of the header
///   record, which holds W3D_NAME_LEN (16 in w3d_file.h) characters,
///   terminator included; a longer name is cut off when written.
/// - Gravity, elasticity and fade time are stored and saved, but
///   ParticleEmitterClass::Create_From_Definition does not pass them on, so
///   changing them in a version 2 asset has no effect through that path. In
///   a version 1 file the fade time is used earlier, as the time of the one
///   end keyframe that ParticleEmitterDefClass::Convert_To_Ver2 builds for
///   colour and for opacity.
/// - Unknown chunk ids inside an emitter chunk are skipped, but the leading
///   chunks (header, user data and info, and for version 2 files the version
///   2 info and the properties) are position-sensitive.
/// - The volume and velocity randomizers draw from one generator that all
///   Vector3Randomizer objects share (a static Random3Class). It is
///   separate from the lockstep logic random values (\ref page_core_frameworks),
///   so the shapes in a definition do not touch the logic random sequence.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The records are plain fixed-width structs from w3d_file.h that the
///   readers fill with a single read of the record size and the writers
///   write whole. The file layout is therefore the C structure layout. A
///   port to another compiler or to 64-bit must keep those structures
///   bit-identical; the in-memory class itself holds raw pointers and has no
///   such constraint.
/// - The shader is stored as a W3D shader record and converted to and from
///   the engine's ShaderClass by W3dUtilityClass::Convert_Shader
///   (\ref grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers). A renderer that does not use
///   ShaderClass's state model has to map the record to its own materials.
/// - The texture is held as a bare file name that ParticleEmitterClass looks
///   up through the texture cache, so texture format and path changes belong
///   to that lookup and not to this class.
/// - Key arrays are allocated with new[] and randomizers with W3DNEW (a
///   plain new outside debug builds), and the callers named above treat the
///   arrays as copies, never shared. A port that wants its own containers
///   can put them behind the getters and setters.
///
/// \see \ref sub_w3d_rendering, \ref page_modding, \ref page_remastering


/// \file WW3D2/part_emt.h
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief The particle emitter render object, and the keyframe track
///        template ParticlePropertyStruct with its copy helper
///        Copy_Emitter_Property_Struct.
///
/// This page describes the header as carried in the BFME 1 reference tree,
/// which follows Zero Hour's WW3D2 header closely: the template originates in
/// Zero Hour's part_emt.h, and the copy differs from it only by a marker
/// comment line and the inline Get_Texture accessor. BFME 2's particle units
/// compile against that copy or against a local shim variant of it, not
/// against Zero Hour's file itself. Only the track copy helper is described
/// here, because ParticleEmitterDefClass is built on it; ParticleEmitterClass
/// is the live emitter that ParticleEmitterDefClass describes.


/// \class ParticleEmitterDefClass
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief The description of one W3D particle emitter: what a W3D emitter
///        chunk stores and what ParticleEmitterClass is created from.
///
/// An emitter in a .w3d file is a set of fixed-size records plus keyframe
/// lists. This class holds them in memory, can read and write them, and
/// hands them to the live emitter or takes them from it. The class is
/// polymorphic: all setters, the chunk readers and writers, the randomizer
/// conversion and the cleanup members are virtual, and so is the destructor.
///
/// \par What it holds
/// The description uses the field names of Zero Hour's header; the BFME 2
/// constructor initialises, default-constructs and clears the same members in
/// the same order, so the layout is the donor's as far as those bodies show.
/// - Identity: a name and a user string (both heap strings the object owns
///   and frees), a user type (Zero Hour's enumeration has a single entry,
///   "Default"), and the file version. None of the bodies in this group acts
///   on the user type or the user string; they are carried through load,
///   copy and save.
/// - The version 1 info record. It includes the texture file name (a fixed
///   260-character field), lifetime, emission rate, maximum emissions, fade
///   time, gravity, elasticity, start velocity and acceleration, a
///   position-random and a velocity-random amount (the version 1 volume
///   sizes), and the fields version 1 used for size and colour, which
///   version 2 replaced with keyframe tracks.
///   ParticleEmitterDefClass::Convert_To_Ver2 turns the colour and size fields
///   into tracks and the two random amounts into box-shaped volume records.
/// - The version 2 info record: burst size, a creation-volume record and a
///   velocity-random record, outward velocity, velocity inheritance, the
///   shader as a W3D shader record, the render mode and the frame mode.
///   The render mode chooses particles or one of the line modes; the frame
///   mode chooses how the texture is divided into a grid of frames, from a
///   single image up to sixteen by sixteen (w3d_file.h).
/// - The extra-info record: the future start time, with padding after it.
/// - The line properties record, used only by the line render modes
///   (w3d_file.h).
/// - The six keyframe tracks (see the group description) and an initial
///   orientation random value that goes with the rotation track.
/// - Two live randomizer objects, one for the creation volume and one for
///   the velocity spread.
/// - The donor header also declares a separate ShaderClass member, placed
///   between the identity fields and the version 1 info record. The
///   constructors default-construct it, so it starts in ShaderClass's default
///   state. No other body documented here reads, writes or copies it: the
///   assignment operator leaves it alone, so a definition made with the copy
///   constructor has the default state in this member whatever the source
///   holds. The shader that is saved and used is the one in the version 2
///   record.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - A definition owns its strings, its six pairs of key arrays and its two
///   randomizers, and frees all of them in its destructor (strings with
///   free, arrays with delete[], randomizers with the global delete).
/// - The setters that take keyframes copy them: the caller keeps its own
///   arrays. ParticleEmitterClass::Build_Definition relies on this and
///   deletes its temporary arrays right after each call. The getters also
///   copy, handing back freshly allocated arrays that the caller must
///   delete, as ParticleEmitterClass::Create_From_Definition does.
/// - The two randomizer setters instead take ownership of the object they
///   are given. The definition's own bodies allocate the randomizers it
///   builds with new (ParticleEmitterDefClass::Create_Randomizer) and
///   release them with the global delete: the destructor, the two setters
///   and the assignment operator all do. The object handed to a setter must
///   therefore be one that the global delete can free. An object that came
///   from a class-specific pool allocator would not be safe to hand in. The
///   randomizer getters return clones.
/// - Copying a definition (the copy constructor and the assignment
///   operator) duplicates all of this and rebuilds the randomizers from the
///   volume records. It is not safe to assign a definition to itself.
///
/// \par Invariants
/// - The class's own code keeps each track's two arrays either null or sized
///   for the track's count. ParticleEmitterDefClass::Free_Props sets the
///   count to zero before it frees the arrays.
/// - The two volume records are the persistent form of the two randomizers.
///   They are what is saved, copied and used to rebuild the objects. The
///   randomizer setters keep them in step with the objects they store; the
///   readers and the assignment operator rebuild the objects from them.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The members documented in this group are reconstructed from the BFME 2
/// retail code; the donor is Zero Hour's GeneralsMD part_ldr.cpp and
/// part_ldr.h, with the format records from w3d_file.h. That the BFME 2
/// layout matches the donor header comes from the constructor's initialising
/// order in the WorldBuilder twin; the roles of the records come from the
/// donor header and its comments.
///
/// \see ParticleEmitterClass::Create_From_Definition,
///      ParticleEmitterClass::Build_Definition,
///      ParticleEmitterDefClass::Load_W3D, ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_W3D


/// \fn ParticleEmitterDefClass::ParticleEmitterDefClass()
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Creates an empty definition: no name or user string, version zero,
///        every record and track cleared, no randomizers, and the separate
///        ShaderClass member in its default state.
///
/// ParticleEmitterClass::Build_Definition creates its result this way and
/// then fills it. The loader path does the same before it reads a chunk (Zero
/// Hour: ParticleEmitterLoaderClass::Load_W3D), and
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Load_W3D calls
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Initialize_To_Ver2 first thing to store the
/// version 2 placeholder values.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Sets the name, user string and both randomizer pointers to null, the
///   version to zero, the user type to the default entry and the initial
///   orientation random value to zero.
/// - Default-constructs the ShaderClass member that the donor header declares
///   ahead of the version 1 info record. The retail body stores ShaderClass's
///   default state there, and the WorldBuilder twin calls a member constructor
///   at the same place. The twin also calls constructors for the version 2
///   info record and the colour track; the retail body stores nothing for
///   those beyond the zero fill. The copy constructor does the same, and the
///   assignment operator never touches the member.
/// - Zero-fills the three info records, the line properties and the six
///   tracks.
///
/// It applies no version 2 defaults, so a bare definition has a zero emission
/// rate, a zero burst size and no volume objects. Because the volume records
/// are zero, they read as a solid box (id zero) of zero size, although no
/// object exists for it.
///
/// \warning The volume getters clone through the pointer without testing it
///          (Zero Hour's header), so calling them on a bare definition
///          crashes. Set the randomizers, load a chunk, or assign from a
///          complete definition first.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's
/// constructor.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::ParticleEmitterDefClass(const ParticleEmitterDefClass &src),
///      ParticleEmitterDefClass::Initialize_To_Ver2


/// \fn ParticleEmitterDefClass::ParticleEmitterDefClass(const ParticleEmitterDefClass &src)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Creates a definition as a deep copy of another one.
///
/// Puts the new object in the same empty state as the default constructor
/// and then assigns from the source, so everything described for
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::operator= applies. No direct caller is identified
/// in BFME 2.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The empty state matters because the assignment starts by freeing and
/// replacing what the object already owns; the strings and the randomizers
/// are null and the tracks are cleared. The initial orientation random value
/// is taken from the source in the initialiser list and again by the
/// assignment. The separate ShaderClass member is default-constructed here,
/// as in the default constructor, and the assignment does not copy it, so
/// the copy's member is in the default state whatever the source holds.
///
/// \param src The definition to copy. Its name and user string are passed to
///            ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Name and
///            ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_User_String, which duplicate a
///            string without testing it for null, so a source that lacks
///            either string is not a supported input.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's
/// copy constructor.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::operator=


/// \fn const ParticleEmitterDefClass &ParticleEmitterDefClass::operator=(const ParticleEmitterDefClass &src)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Replaces this definition's contents with a deep copy of another
///        definition's.
///
/// The copy constructor calls it. It is the body to read for what a copy of
/// a definition carries and for the order in which the dependent parts are
/// rebuilt.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The name, user string and user type are copied through their virtual
///   setters and the version directly.
/// - The version 1 info, version 2 info, extra-info and line-properties
///   records are copied as raw bytes.
/// - ParticleEmitterDefClass::Free_Props releases this object's old tracks,
///   then each of the six tracks is deep-copied with
///   Copy_Emitter_Property_Struct, and the initial orientation random value
///   is copied.
/// - Both randomizers are deleted and rebuilt with
///   ParticleEmitterDefClass::Create_Randomizer from the volume records that
///   were just copied. They are not cloned from the source's objects, so a
///   randomizer is carried over only as far as its record describes it.
/// - The separate ShaderClass member that the constructors default-construct
///   is not copied. The shader that a copy carries is the one in the copied
///   version 2 info record.
///
/// \warning The function does not guard against assignment to itself. The
///          name and user string would be freed before they are copied, and
///          the tracks would be emptied before they are copied, and so lost.
///
/// \param src The definition to copy from.
/// \return This definition.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's
/// assignment operator.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::ParticleEmitterDefClass(const ParticleEmitterDefClass &src),
///      ParticleEmitterDefClass::Free_Props,
///      ParticleEmitterDefClass::Create_Randomizer


/// \fn void ParticleEmitterDefClass::Free_Props(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Empties all six keyframe tracks and releases their arrays.
///
/// The destructor, the assignment operator,
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Initialize_To_Ver2,
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Convert_To_Ver2 and the properties reader all
/// call it. The reader does so before it reads a chunk, so a track is never
/// left holding stale arrays.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Sets the keyframe count of all six tracks to zero first, then deletes
/// each of the twelve arrays (a times array and a values array per track)
/// if it is not null and sets the pointer to null. The start value and the
/// random-variation amount of each track, and the initial orientation random
/// value, are left as they were.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::operator=, Copy_Emitter_Property_Struct


/// \fn void ParticleEmitterDefClass::Normalize_Filename(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Strips any directory part from the stored texture file name.
///
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Texture_Filename calls it after storing a
/// name, so a definition built from a live emitter keeps only the file name.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Copies the stored name to a local buffer, finds the last backslash in it,
/// and if there is one copies the part after it back over the stored name.
/// A name that ends in a backslash becomes empty. Only the backslash counts
/// as a separator; a forward slash is left in the name. Names the loader
/// reads from a chunk are not passed through this function.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The stored name is therefore a bare file name for any definition produced
/// through Set_Texture_Filename, whatever directory the author used. How the
/// texture cache resolves that name to a file is outside this class.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Texture_Filename


/// \fn void ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Texture_Filename(const char *pname)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Stores the emitter's texture file name, keeping only the file part.
///
/// ParticleEmitterClass::Build_Definition calls it with the live emitter's
/// texture name, when the emitter has a texture. The name is read back
/// through the definition's texture-name getter by
/// ParticleEmitterClass::Create_From_Definition, which looks the texture up
/// when the name is not empty.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Copies the name into the fixed-size field of the version 1 info record
/// and calls ParticleEmitterDefClass::Normalize_Filename on it.
///
/// \warning The copy is not bounds-checked. A name longer than the field
///          overruns it.
///
/// \param pname The texture file name, possibly with a directory. Must not
///              be null.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Normalize_Filename


/// \fn void ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Velocity(const Vector3 &value)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Sets the emitter's start velocity.
///
/// ParticleEmitterClass::Build_Definition passes the live emitter's start
/// velocity. ParticleEmitterClass::Create_From_Definition reads it back with
/// the matching getter. The value lives in the version 1 info record, which
/// version 2 files keep for this and for acceleration.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Copies the three components into the record. This class does not define
/// the unit. The live emitter's start-velocity getter multiplies its stored
/// value by 1000, which suggests that the definition's value is per second
/// and the emitter's own is per millisecond; that is inferred from the header
/// view that Build_Definition is compiled against, not from this body.
///
/// \param value The velocity vector to store.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is the inline
/// setter in Zero Hour's header, which has an out-of-line copy because the
/// function is virtual.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Acceleration


/// \fn void ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Acceleration(const Vector3 &value)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Sets the emitter's constant acceleration.
///
/// The twin of ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Velocity. Build_Definition passes
/// the live emitter's acceleration, and Create_From_Definition reads it back
/// with the getter.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Copies the three components into the version 1 info record.
///
/// \param value The acceleration vector to store.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is the inline
/// setter in Zero Hour's header.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Velocity


/// \fn void ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Creation_Volume(Vector3Randomizer *randomizer)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Takes ownership of a randomizer as the shape particles are
///        created in, and records its parameters for saving.
///
/// ParticleEmitterClass::Build_Definition passes in the clone it gets from
/// the live emitter, so after the call the definition owns an object that is
/// independent of the emitter. When ParticleEmitterClass::Create_From_Definition
/// later asks for the volume it receives a further clone.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Deletes the randomizer the definition already holds, stores the new
/// pointer, and if the pointer is not null writes the object's shape id and
/// parameters into the persistent creation-volume record with
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Initialize_Randomizer_Struct. The record is what
/// Save_W3D writes and the assignment operator copies, so it must follow the
/// object. A null argument deletes the old object and leaves the record
/// holding the old values, so a later copy would rebuild the old shape.
///
/// \warning The definition's own bodies release a randomizer with the global
///          delete, and the one passed in will be released that way too. It
///          must therefore be an object the global delete can free (an
///          object from a class-specific pool allocator would not be safe
///          to hand in), and it must not be shared. The caller must not use
///          or free it afterwards.
///
/// \param randomizer The shape to adopt; the definition takes ownership.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Velocity_Random,
///      ParticleEmitterDefClass::Initialize_Randomizer_Struct


/// \fn void ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Velocity_Random(Vector3Randomizer *randomizer)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Takes ownership of a randomizer as the spread added to the start
///        velocity, and records its parameters for saving.
///
/// The twin of ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Creation_Volume, working on the
/// velocity-random record instead of the creation-volume one.
/// ParticleEmitterClass::Build_Definition passes the live emitter's cloned
/// velocity randomizer.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Deletes the randomizer the definition holds, stores the new pointer, and,
/// when it is not null, fills the velocity-random record from it with
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Initialize_Randomizer_Struct. As in
/// Set_Creation_Volume, a null argument leaves the record unchanged. The
/// live emitter's getter scales the clone it hands to Build_Definition by
/// 1000, the same factor as for the start velocity (see
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Velocity), so the stored spread is in the
/// definition's units.
///
/// \warning Ownership and null-handling are as for
///          ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Creation_Volume.
///
/// \param randomizer The shape to adopt; the definition takes ownership.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Creation_Volume


/// \fn void ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Size_Keyframes(ParticlePropertyStruct<float> &keyframes)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Replaces the particle size track with a copy of the given one.
///
/// ParticleEmitterClass::Build_Definition copies the live emitter's size
/// track in with it. The same track is written by
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Size_Keyframes, inside the properties chunk.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Deletes the track's two arrays, then deep-copies the argument over the
/// track with Copy_Emitter_Property_Struct: the start value, random
/// variation and count come across and fresh arrays are allocated and filled.
/// The caller keeps its own arrays and still owns them.
///
/// \param keyframes The track to copy. It is passed by non-const reference
///                  but not changed.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Opacity_Keyframes,
///      ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Size_Keyframes


/// \fn void ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Opacity_Keyframes(ParticlePropertyStruct<float> &keyframes)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Replaces the particle opacity track with a copy of the given one.
///
/// Same copy rules as ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Size_Keyframes. The opacity
/// track is written by ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Opacity_Keyframes.
/// In Zero Hour's version 1 conversion opacity comes from an 8-bit alpha
/// divided by 255, which suggests a zero-to-one scale; the BFME 2 bodies
/// documented here do not show the scale.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Deletes the track's two arrays, then deep-copies the argument with
/// Copy_Emitter_Property_Struct. The caller's arrays are untouched.
///
/// \param keyframes The track to copy; not changed.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Size_Keyframes,
///      ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Opacity_Keyframes


/// \fn void ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Frame_Keyframes(ParticlePropertyStruct<float> &keyframes)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Replaces the texture frame track with a copy of the given one.
///
/// The frame track animates which sub-image of the texture a particle shows
/// (w3d_file.h: frame keys for UV-based frame animation), within the grid that
/// the definition's frame mode selects. It is stored in a chunk of its own,
/// not in the properties chunk.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Deletes the track's two arrays and deep-copies the argument with
/// Copy_Emitter_Property_Struct, as for
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Size_Keyframes.
///
/// \param keyframes The track to copy; not changed.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's.
/// The role of the frame track comes from the file-format header.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Blur_Time_Keyframes


/// \fn void ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Blur_Time_Keyframes(ParticlePropertyStruct<float> &keyframes)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Replaces the blur-time track with a copy of the given one.
///
/// The file-format header describes this track as the length of the tail
/// for line groups, so it matters to the line render modes. It is stored in
/// a chunk of its own.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Deletes the track's two arrays and deep-copies the argument with
/// Copy_Emitter_Property_Struct, as for
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Size_Keyframes.
///
/// \param keyframes The track to copy; not changed.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's.
/// The meaning of the track comes from the file-format header.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Frame_Keyframes


/// \fn void ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Rotation_Keyframes(ParticlePropertyStruct<float> &keyframes, float orient_rnd)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Replaces the rotation track with a copy of the given one and sets
///        the random initial orientation that goes with it.
///
/// The rotation track holds rotational velocity keyframes; the file-format
/// header gives the unit as rotations per second, and the orientation
/// random value in rotations, where 1.0 is a full turn. Both are stored with
/// the rotation chunk. ParticleEmitterClass::Build_Definition passes the live
/// emitter's initial orientation random value as the second argument.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Deletes the track's two arrays, deep-copies the argument with
/// Copy_Emitter_Property_Struct, and stores the orientation value. The
/// caller's arrays are untouched.
///
/// \param keyframes  The track to copy; not changed.
/// \param orient_rnd Random initial orientation, in rotations (1.0 is a
///                   full turn).
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's.
/// The units come from the file-format header's comments.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Frame_Keyframes


/// \fn void ParticleEmitterDefClass::Initialize_To_Ver2(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Resets the info records and tracks and stores placeholder version 2
///        values, before a chunk is read into it.
///
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Load_W3D calls it before it reads anything, so
/// that every record has a defined value when the readers start. A version 1
/// file is later converted by ParticleEmitterDefClass::Convert_To_Ver2, and a
/// version 2 file's version 2 info reader replaces the whole version 2 record
/// with the file's own. The values stored here are therefore placeholders
/// that the readers replace, not defaults that a loaded emitter keeps.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Zero-fills the version 1 info, version 2 info and extra-info records.
/// - Sets the burst size to one, outward velocity and velocity inheritance to
///   zero, and the shader record to the additive sprite preset (through
///   W3dUtilityClass::Convert_Shader, see \ref grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers).
/// - Sets both volume records to the solid box id with zero values.
/// - Releases all tracks with ParticleEmitterDefClass::Free_Props.
///
/// It does not create the randomizer objects, so after this call they are
/// whatever they were; for a fresh definition, null. The line properties
/// and the name are not touched.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Do not read these values as what an asset gets when it leaves a field out.
/// A version 2 file that omits the version 2 info chunk fails to load
/// instead of inheriting them. A version 2 file that has the chunk gets its
/// own values: the reader clears the record and overwrites all of it. A
/// version 1 file gets values from ParticleEmitterDefClass::Convert_To_Ver2,
/// which sets the burst size to one, always ends with the additive sprite
/// preset (in BFME 2 the texture-alpha predicate it calls always returns
/// false, so the alpha sprite preset that the Zero Hour source picks from the
/// texture format is never chosen), and derives the two volumes from the
/// version 1 position-random and velocity-random amounts. So after a
/// successful load, the burst size, shader and volumes come from the file or
/// from the conversion, not from the placeholders stored here. The render
/// mode and frame mode of a version 1 file keep the zero fill, because the
/// conversion does not set them.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Free_Props


/// \fn Vector3Randomizer *ParticleEmitterDefClass::Create_Randomizer(W3dVolumeRandomizerStruct &info)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Builds the randomizer object that a volume record describes.
///
/// The volume record is the saved form of a spawn shape or a velocity
/// spread: a shape id and up to three values. This turns it into the live
/// object that picks random vectors. It is used wherever a definition's
/// randomizers have to be (re)built from records: the assignment operator,
/// the version 2 info reader and the version 1 conversion.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// A switch on the shape id, with ids in the order of the Vector3Randomizer
/// class-id enumeration:
/// - 0, solid box: the three values are the box extents on x, y and z.
///   The box is centred on the origin, and a vector is picked uniformly
///   within plus or minus each extent.
/// - 1, solid sphere: the first value is the radius.
/// - 2, hollow sphere: the first value is the radius. Vectors lie on the
///   surface.
/// - 3, solid cylinder: the first value is the extent along the cylinder's
///   axis and the second is the radius. The axis is the x axis; the circle
///   lies in y and z.
///
/// Any other id creates nothing and returns null. Each object is allocated
/// with W3DNEW, which is a plain new outside debug builds; the randomizer
/// constructors clamp negative values to zero.
///
/// \param info The volume record to read.
/// \return A new randomizer, owned by the caller, or null for an unknown
///         shape id.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The shape ids and the meaning of the value fields per shape are part of
/// the W3D emitter format. A definition that holds a null result is not
/// usable: the volume getters clone without a test.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's.
/// The shapes' behaviour comes from the BFME 2 bodies of the randomizer
/// classes (their Get_Vector functions and constructors).
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Initialize_Randomizer_Struct,
///      ParticleEmitterDefClass::operator=


/// \fn void ParticleEmitterDefClass::Initialize_Randomizer_Struct(const Vector3Randomizer &randomizer, W3dVolumeRandomizerStruct &info)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Fills a volume record from a randomizer object: the inverse of
///        ParticleEmitterDefClass::Create_Randomizer.
///
/// The randomizer setters call it so that the persistent record always
/// matches the live object that was adopted.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Writes the object's shape id into the record, then asks the matching
/// concrete class for its parameters:
/// - solid box: extents into the three values;
/// - solid sphere and hollow sphere: the radius into the first value;
/// - solid cylinder: the axial extent into the first value and the radius
///   into the second.
///
/// Values that a shape does not use are left as they were. An id it does
/// not know is written to the record but leaves the values alone. The
/// reserved words in the record are never written.
///
/// \param randomizer The object to describe.
/// \param info       [out] The record to fill.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Create_Randomizer,
///      ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Creation_Volume


/// \fn bool ParticleEmitterDefClass::Read_Color_Keyframe(ChunkLoadClass &chunk_load, float *key_time, Vector3 *value)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Reads one colour keyframe record from the current chunk and
///        converts it to a time and a colour vector.
///
/// The properties reader calls it once for the start colour, passing no time
/// pointer, and then once for each further keyframe. The record is a time
/// and an RGBA colour of four bytes.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Reads one colour record of exactly the record size. If the read is
/// complete, it stores the time through the first output when that pointer
/// is not null, and the colour through the second when that is not null. Each
/// of red, green and blue is converted by dividing by 255. The alpha byte is
/// ignored. A short read stores nothing.
///
/// \warning The properties reader does not test the result, so a truncated
///          properties chunk loads as if it were complete, with the
///          remaining keys unwritten.
///
/// \param chunk_load The chunk to read from, positioned at a colour record.
/// \param key_time   [out] Receives the key time; may be null.
/// \param value      [out] Receives the colour; may be null.
/// \return True if a whole record was read, false on a short read.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's.
/// The caller's use comes from the BFME 2 properties reader.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Color_Keyframes,
///      ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Props


/// \fn bool ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Props(ChunkSaveClass &chunk_save)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Writes the properties chunk: the colour, opacity and size tracks.
///
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_W3D calls it fifth, after the version 2
/// info and before the line properties. The reader is the counterpart that
/// runs in ParticleEmitterDefClass::Load_W3D for version 2 files.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Begins the properties chunk. If that fails, returns false.
/// - Fills a properties record: the three counts, each as the track's
///   further-keyframe count plus one; the opacity and size random
///   variations as floats; and the colour random variation as an RGBA colour
///   of four bytes whose alpha byte is left zero, converted the same way as
///   keyframe colours (see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Color_Keyframes).
/// - Writes the record. If it is written whole, writes the colour, opacity
///   and size keyframes in that order with
///   ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Color_Keyframes,
///   ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Opacity_Keyframes and
///   ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Size_Keyframes, stopping at the first
///   that fails.
/// - Always ends the chunk it began, so the output stays balanced.
///
/// Rotation, frame and blur-time tracks are not written here; each has its
/// own chunk and writer. The record's reserved words are written as zero.
///
/// \param chunk_save The chunk writer.
/// \return True only if the chunk was begun and the record and all three
///         tracks were written completely.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's version returns WW3DErrorType. BFME 2's returns a bool.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic and the record
/// layout are Zero Hour's (w3d_file.h). The counting convention is
/// confirmed on the read side by the BFME 2 properties reader, which
/// subtracts one.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_W3D


/// \fn bool ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Extra_Info(ChunkSaveClass &chunk_save)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Writes the extra-info chunk, which carries the future start time.
///
/// The last chunk ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_W3D writes. The loader
/// accepts it in any position after the leading chunks. The file-format
/// header describes the future start time as whether to start a particle in
/// the future; how ParticleEmitterClass uses the value is not established
/// here.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Begins the chunk, fills a zeroed extra-info record with the definition's
/// future start time, writes it, and ends the chunk whether or not the write
/// succeeded. The rest of the record is padding and is written as zero.
///
/// \param chunk_save The chunk writer.
/// \return True if the chunk was begun and the record was written whole.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's version returns WW3DErrorType. BFME 2's returns a bool.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's.
/// The meaning of the field comes from the file-format header.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_W3D


/// \fn bool ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Color_Keyframes(ChunkSaveClass &chunk_save)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Writes the colour track as a series of keyframe records inside
///        the properties chunk.
///
/// Called only by ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Props. Opacity and size use
/// the same scheme in ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Opacity_Keyframes and
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Size_Keyframes.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The first record is the track's start colour with time zero.
/// - One record follows for each further keyframe, with its time and colour.
///   Writing stops at the first record that is not written whole.
/// - A colour record is a time and an RGBA colour of four bytes. Each channel
///   is the float value times 255, with negative values clamped to zero,
///   truncated to a byte. The alpha byte is always zero.
/// - The result is true only if every record was written whole.
///
/// The clamp means that the negative last colour which the properties reader
/// creates for a black ending with a random spread is saved as black. The
/// conversion truncates and does not round, so a colour is quantised
/// downward to a multiple of 1/255. There is no upper clamp, so a component
/// above one does not saturate.
///
/// \param chunk_save The chunk writer, inside the properties chunk.
/// \return True if every record was written whole.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's version returns WW3DErrorType. BFME 2's returns a bool.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the order of writes and
/// the colour conversion are Zero Hour's. The clamping effect on the reader's
/// negative colour is derived from the BFME 2 properties reader and the
/// conversion macro.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Read_Color_Keyframe,
///      ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Props


/// \fn bool ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Opacity_Keyframes(ChunkSaveClass &chunk_save)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Writes the opacity track as keyframe records inside the
///        properties chunk.
///
/// Called only by ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Props, after the colour
/// track. The scheme is that of ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Color_Keyframes
/// with a float value in place of the colour.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Writes a first record with time zero and the start opacity, then one
/// record per further keyframe with its time and value, stopping at the
/// first that is not written whole. Values are written as floats without
/// conversion.
///
/// \param chunk_save The chunk writer, inside the properties chunk.
/// \return True if every record was written whole.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's version returns WW3DErrorType. BFME 2's returns a bool.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Size_Keyframes,
///      ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Opacity_Keyframes


/// \fn bool ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Size_Keyframes(ChunkSaveClass &chunk_save)
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_w3d_particle_emitter_def
/// \brief Writes the size track as keyframe records inside the properties
///        chunk.
///
/// Called only by ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Props, last of the three.
/// The scheme is identical to
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Opacity_Keyframes.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Writes a first record with time zero and the start size, then one record
/// per further keyframe, stopping at the first that is not written whole.
///
/// \param chunk_save The chunk writer, inside the properties chunk.
/// \return True if every record was written whole.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's version returns WW3DErrorType. BFME 2's returns a bool.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body reconstructed from the BFME 2 retail code; the logic is Zero Hour's.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::Save_Opacity_Keyframes,
///      ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Size_Keyframes


/// \fn void Copy_Emitter_Property_Struct(ParticlePropertyStruct<T> &dest, const ParticlePropertyStruct<T> &src)
/// \brief Deep-copies one keyframe track into another, allocating new key
///        arrays for the destination.
///
/// The helper behind every keyframe setter and getter of
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass, in the instantiations for float tracks (opacity,
/// size, rotation, frame, blur time) and for Vector3 tracks (colour). They
/// come from one template and are identical in source, but the compiled forms
/// differ: the Vector3 instantiation also runs array constructors on the
/// values array it allocates (through the compiler's array-constructor
/// iterator) and carries an exception handler for that, which the float
/// instantiation has neither of.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Copies the start value, the random-variation amount and the keyframe count,
/// and sets the destination's arrays to null. If the count is above zero,
/// allocates a times array and a values array of that length with plain
/// new[], and copies the source's entries with a raw memory copy, so the
/// value type must be safe to copy that way.
///
/// The function does not free whatever arrays the destination already held.
/// Callers that overwrite a populated track free it first, as
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Free_Props and the setters do. A getter that
/// calls it on a fresh structure leaves the caller to free the arrays with
/// delete[].
///
/// \param dest [out] The track to fill.
/// \param src  The track to copy.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The template is Zero Hour's, from part_emt.h; the BFME 2 code contains
/// the float and Vector3 instantiations, and the callers named above are
/// BFME 2 bodies.
/// \see ParticleEmitterDefClass::operator=,
///      ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Size_Keyframes
